f 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415474 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f920ea7f-06a7-52e9-8586-d0e6b44af6b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415477 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n- $A$ has two proportional rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415479 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415480 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415480 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0922e6fb-1dc5-5a88-a985-bdf2643cb465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415480 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415481 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415481 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73a434b3-1e72-5102-a3b4-f3e485e5088a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415481 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7fde0eea-1a95-5e36-bc1d-53171442da99" #edb/ref 17592